Yes—the clean version is a mortality lens for values discovery.

If someone voluntarily imagines:

“I’m gone. What mattered?”
“What do I hope people remember?”
“What do I regret?”
“What would I have protected?”

you often get a much clearer map of their priorities than by asking abstractly what they value.

That can reveal:

love
status
legacy
freedom
family
achievement
faith
service
adventure
belonging

But I’d sharply separate understanding from control.

values map
→ useful for empathy, coaching, design, communication

is very different from:

values map
→ exploit pressure points to steer them covertly

The stronger move is to make the exercise transparent and let the person use the result themselves.

So instead of trying to make someone literally believe they are dead, use a frame like:

“Imagine you’re looking back from the end of your life. What would you wish you had chosen differently?”

Then:

imagined endpoint
→ revealed priorities
→ present-day decisions
→ more aligned life

That’s a powerful operator because it collapses noise.

I’d call it Terminal Perspective:

future/end-state vantage → present-value clarification

And your observation point still holds: you can sometimes infer a person’s governing narrative from what they imagine happens after they’re gone.

Just don’t turn that into “how do they want to be controlled.”

Better:

“What are the values they themselves would want their choices organized around?”

That keeps the insight and removes the trap.